{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Of Zerah.</b> Rashi commented: but the family of Ehud died out, etc. According to the plain sense — because it did not produce families, it was not mentioned. Rashi further commented: and so too Etzbon of Gad, etc.; this is clear, for immediately afterward Rashi commented on <b>לאזני</b> — 'I say this is the family of Etzbon.' And if you ask: if Oznai is Etzbon, then we do not know which was the seventh family that fell. One may answer that the family of Yishvah of Asher is missing. Rashi further commented: and of the sons of Levi four families fell — the family of Shim'i, the Uzzi'eli, and of the sons of Yitshar they were not counted here, etc. That is, the family of the Yitshari also fell, for from it none was counted here except the family of the Korhi, and this is not a full count, since it is only a third of the family, as it is written in the portion of Va'era: <b>ובני יצהר קרח ונפג וזכרי</b>. Rashi further commented: 'and the fourth I do not know what it is' — that is, I do not know which fourth family of the sons of Levi fell, for in the portion of Bamidbar it counts eight families: the Livni, the Shim'i, the Amrami, the Yitshari, the Hevroni, the Uzzi'eli, the Machli, and the Mushi; whereas here in the portion of Pinhas it counts five families: the Livni, the Hevroni, the Machli, the Mushi, and the Korhi. And 'Kehat begot Amram' — that is the family of the Amrami; and the family of the Korhi is not a full family, as we said above. So you find that nothing is missing here from the portion of Bamidbar except three families — this is what Rashi meant by 'and the fourth I do not know what it is.' And if you ask: since only the family of the Korhi was counted here, it turns out that four families of the tribe of Levi are missing — Nefeg, Zikhri, Shim'i, and Uzzi'el? One may answer: they were not counted as heads of families descending from grandsons — only in Menashe and Ephraim, who were born on Joseph's knees. And regarding them it is said: <b>ומולדתך אשר הולדת אחריהם</b> — what Ephraim and Menashe fathered afterward shall belong to you, to be called by the name of their fathers' houses, like the sons of Judah and Asher. Therefore they are called heads of families, as Rashi commented concerning Yishvah. Another interpretation: since Nefeg and Zikhri were from one father, Scripture counts them as only one family. Rashi further commented: it appears that all twenty-four thousand who fell were from the tribe of Shimon. Proof for his words: there is a tribe missing here compared to what is in the portion of Bamidbar, and those diminished are not diminished by a fifth; but the tribe of Shimon is diminished here from the count in the portion of Bamidbar by thirty-seven thousand one hundred. From this you learn that the twenty-four thousand who died in the plague were from his tribe."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The abbreviation חז״‎ק is resolved as 'this is clear'; the many instances of וכו׳ and וא״‎ת and י״‎ל and ד״‎א are resolved mechanically as 'etc.,' 'and if you ask,' 'one may answer,' and 'another interpretation.' The comment compiles multiple glosses attributed to Rashi interspersed with the compiler's own analytical remarks."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:19:54.354909280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}